        "title": "The Reciprocal Ratios",
        "renderer": "manim",
        "narration": "Beyond Sine, Cosine, and Tangent, there are three more trigonometric ratios. These are called the reciprocal ratios because they are simply the reciprocals, or the 'flipped versions', of the first three. The reciprocal of Sine is called Cosecant, written as csc A. Since sin A is Opposite over Hypotenuse, csc A is Hypotenuse over Opposite. You can think of it as 1 divided by sin A. The reciprocal of Cosine is called Secant, written as sec A. Since cos A is Adjacent over Hypotenuse, sec A flips this to become Hypotenuse over Adjacent. This is the same as 1 divided by cos A. Finally, the reciprocal of Tangent is Cotangent, written as cot A. Tan A is Opposite over Adjacent, so cot A is Adjacent over Opposite. And just like the others, it's equal to 1 divided by tan A. While SOH CAH TOA helps with the main three, understanding these reciprocal pairs is essential for solving more complex trigonometric equations later on.",
