        "section_type": "example",
        "id": 7,
        "title": "Example 2: Proving Angles Equal",
        "renderer": "manim",
        "narration": "In this example, we have a more theoretical problem. We are given two acute angles, B and Q, such that sin B is equal to sin Q. Our goal is to prove that angle B must be equal to angle Q. To start, let's visualize this with two separate right-angled triangles: triangle ABC, right-angled at C, and triangle PQR, right-angled at R. From the given information, sin B = sin Q. Writing this in terms of the sides of our triangles, we get AC over AB equals PR over PQ. We can rearrange this proportion to say that the ratio of the opposite sides, AC over PR, is equal to the ratio of the hypotenuses, AB over PQ. Let's call this common ratio 'k'. So, AC equals k times PR, and AB equals k times PQ. Now, let's find the third side of each triangle using the Pythagorean theorem. For triangle ABC, the adjacent side BC is the square root of AB squared minus AC squared. Substituting our expressions in terms of 'k', this becomes the square root of k-squared PQ-squared minus k-squared PR-squared. We can factor out k-squared and take it out of the square root, leaving us with BC equals k times the square root of PQ-squared minus PR-squared. For triangle PQR, the adjacent side QR is simply the square root of PQ-squared minus PR-squared. Now, if we look at the ratio of the adjacent sides, BC over QR, we see that the square root terms cancel out, leaving us with just 'k'. This is a fantastic result! It means that AC/PR = AB/PQ = BC/QR = k. Since all three corresponding sides of the triangles are in the same proportion, the triangles ABC and PQR are similar by the SSS similarity criterion. And if two triangles are similar, their corresponding angles must be equal. Therefore, angle B is equal to angle Q.",
        "narration_segments": [
          {
            "id": 1,
            "text": "In this example, we have a more theoretical problem. We are given two acute angles, B and Q, such that sin B is equal to sin Q. Our goal is to prove that angle B must be equal to angle Q.",
