            "scene": 1,
            "concept_title": "The Right Triangle's Anatomy",
            "description": "A student identifies the sides of a triangle in a real-world context.",
            "wan_prompt": "Flat vector illustration, bright colors, Indian educational textbook style, purple and blue gradient background. A student in a blue school uniform stands at the corner of a right-angled park. An arrow points from the student across the park, labeled 'Opposite'. An arrow points along the path next to the student, labeled 'Adjacent'. A big arrow points along the diagonal path, labeled 'Hypotenuse'. The student looks thoughtfully at the labels.",
            "narration": "Remember that everything in basic trigonometry starts with a right-angled triangle. From any corner angle, we have three special sides. The side directly across from you is the Opposite. The side right next to you, forming the angle, is the Adjacent. And the longest side, the diagonal path across from the right angle, is always the Hypotenuse. Knowing which side is which is the crucial first step for every single problem."
          },
          {
            "scene": 2,
            "concept_title": "SOH CAH TOA in Action",
            "description": "A kite flyer uses trigonometry to estimate height.",
            "wan_prompt": "Flat vector illustration, bright colors, Indian educational textbook style, purple and blue gradient background. A young girl is flying a kite. A right triangle is superimposed, with the kite at the top vertex, the girl at another, and a point on the ground below the kite as the third. The kite string is the Hypotenuse. The angle of the string is labeled 'θ'. A thought bubble shows 'SOH: sin(θ) = Height / String Length'.",
            "narration": "The core of our calculations is the mnemonic SOH CAH TOA. It's our formula sheet. If you know the length of a kite string (the Hypotenuse) and the angle it makes with the ground, you can find the kite's height (the Opposite) using Sine. Sine is Opposite over Hypotenuse. This simple idea lets us measure things we can't possibly reach, just by knowing an angle and one distance."
          },
          {
            "scene": 3,
